<p>This Halloween marks the 100th anniversary of <a href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=Bj6ulLJGPB8">the death of famed escape artist Harry Houdini</a>. Today, most of us know more about the manner of that death than we do about any of <a href="https://www.openculture.com/2013/11/watch-houdini-escape-from-a-straight-jacket.html">t</a><a href="https://www.openculture.com/2013/11/watch-houdini-escape-from-a-straight-jacket.html">he feats of escape artistry</a> that made him famous in the first place. As the story goes, he’d mastered his own body to the extent that he could tighten his abdominal muscles so as to withstand even the hardest punch to the gut. Or at least that’s the image he let circulate: taking it as a challenge, a student and amateur boxer turned up and offered his fist to Houdini as an unexpected test. A week and a half later, the great escapologist was dead of peritonitis.</p>
<p>It seemed that the blow he sustained had ruptured Houdini’s already-inflamed appendix, and that remained the widely accepted, if not medically airtight, explanation for nearly a century. Yet there was always something too dramatically neat in the tale of a man famed for defying death being done in by nothing more than a thoughtless young man’s sucker punch. Generations of enthusiasts have offered explanations of their own, some more plausible than others, but few investigations into this quasi-legendary demise have been conducted as seriously as the one undertaken in our time by retired urologist Larrian Gillespie, author of <a href="https://link.amazon/B07SJmec2"><em>Houdini: The Man Who Died Twice</em>.</a></p>
<div class="oc-center-da">
<div data-fuse="22871471544"></div>
</div>

<p>When she had the chance to examine his death certificate, Gillespie noticed apparent irregularities that led her down the rabbit hole to “a paper trail of misstatements and actions by more than a dozen people close to Houdini, including relatives and employees,” <a href="https://www.nytimes.com/2026/10/20/arts/houdini-death-mystery.html?unlocked_article_code=1.LVE.vPyz.Q20UB0l8YigY&amp;smid=url-share">writes the New York&nbsp;<em>Times</em>’ Robin Pogrebin</a>, “including a conspiracy to change his official death date to October 31st, fixing his memory to Halloween.” According to her reconstruction of events, Houdini actually died the day before: not of appendicitis, and not as a result of the young man’s punch. “Rather, she argues, the culprit was a bowel obstruction, brought on by a fibrous mass of chop suey that Houdini ate on Oct. 28.”</p>
