<p>Emergency surgery took place the next day, but it was too late. “Septic shock set in, Houdini’s fever spiked to 105 and his rapid breathing led to muscle spasms and unconsciousness.” He was operated on again the day after, and “Gillespie’s research into the death certificates indicates that the magician died that afternoon at 1:26 p.m.” Gillespie argues that the lack of a proper autopsy helped conceal possible medical malpractice and allowed Houdini’s widow to collect a double-indemnity payment for what was treated as an accidental death. Even if accepted, her theory probably won’t erase the cultural link between Houdini and Halloween. But the theory does add a new dimension of irony to his life story: the man who could survive a <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Chinese_Water_Torture_Cell">Chinese water torture cell</a> proved to be no match for American Chinese food.</p>
